+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document instead of the whole new version. The library can apply a JSON Patch (`applyPatch`) but cannot produce one.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Add an exported function `createPatch(from, to)` that returns an RFC 6902 JSON Patch which, applied to `from`, yields a document equal to `to`.
+- The patch is minimal in size of description: unchanged parts are never mentioned; objects and arrays are compared recursively; an array item that is inserted or removed yields one `add` or `remove` and leaves its neighbours untouched; an item that moves within an array yields one `move` and leaves the items in between untouched.
+- Neither input is mutated, and the patch does not share structure with the inputs.
+- Not breaking: purely additive export. No existing behaviour changes.
+
+**Governing standards:** RFC 6902 §3, §4.1–§4.4 (operations produced), §5 (application); RFC 6901 §3 and §5 (pointer syntax and escaping); RFC 8259 (JSON values, equality of members regardless of order).
+
+**Out of scope:** producing `copy` or `test` operations; validating that inputs are JSON values; guaranteeing the globally shortest possible patch (the output is minimal for the cases above, not an optimal edit script); JSON Merge Patch generation.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+- `json-patch-diff`: producing a JSON Patch that transforms one JSON document into another.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+<!-- None -->
+
+## Impact
+
+- `src/index.ts`: new exported `createPatch` (reuses existing pointer, clone and equality helpers).
+- `test/diff.test.ts`: new tests, including round-trip checks through `applyPatch`.
+- No new dependencies; no `package.json` change.
